ose comparison videos and by  the way this video is going to be fully  dedicated to going deep into the m1  ultra chip architecture and explaining  things that apple didn't mention at the  event and we're going to have another  video coming soon dedicated to the mac  studio so stay tuned for that and now  with that said let's dig deep into the  m1 ultra chip first off this chip is  absolutely  massive it's going to be so long that  it'll essentially stretch across the  entire max studio logic board which is  actually a very good thing for cooling  performance and like i said before apple  is using an interconnect section on the  bottom of each m1 max die to connect  both dyes into one and the best part of  all is that apple did all of the  groundwork to make it behave like a  single die chip and this is extremely  important because it means that  developers don't have to do any work at  all to take full advantage of the m1  ultra unlike many of the dual xeon intel  chips out there that have issues with  software support where some programs  aren't able to take full advantage of  those dual cpu systems but that will not  be an issue at all with the m1 ultra and  we know that for sure because apple says  that the interposer between both dies  has 2.5 terabytes per second of  bandwidth thanks to the ultra fusion  fabric which is absolutely mind-blowing  and it basically ensures that you won't  have any bottlenecks between the dies  they'll basically run at full  performance as long as the cooling  system can keep up and we basically know  that for sure because apple is rating it  at up to 800 gigabytes per second dram  bandwidth which is exactly two times  more than the m1 max which means that  apple isn't expecting any bottlenecks at  all even with the massive up to 128 gigs  of unified memory being shared between  the interposer with both dies thanks to  the ultra fusion fabric this is a huge  deal because this means that apps that  require a ton of vram like 3d vide